;;; A theory built on a theory, imported by an entry that does not name the
;;; lower one's: the lower theory's rules stay behind, and the loader says
;;; so in one line — M1f S1f.6.25, E-20 of P1f.6's `findings.md`.
;;;
;;; `upper_without_entry.ein` imports `lower` by its entry and uses it; its
;;; own entry, `upper`, names its two rules and not `lower`. The closure of
;;; `(upper)` follows the names those rules write: the relations `ask` and
;;; `double-of`, whose declarations come, and no rule of the lower theory.
;;; That is the closure rule as `01_grammar.md` § Import states it: a
;;; selection is a selection. So `(ask a)` is derived and never answered, and
;;; `ask` and `double-of`, which the lower theory's entry would have closed,
;;; are open: left to itself the search would guess their facts. This
;;; program states no hypothesis, to keep the answer small: R {∅}, and the
;;; goal has no row.
;;;
;;; The symptom, an unanswered request, is far from the cause, an entry
;;; name missing two files away, so the loader prints, on every command:
;;;
;;;   note: tests/imports/02_nested_import_left_behind.ein:28:2: (import
;;;   upper_without_entry :symbols (upper)) brings the declaration of
;;;   double-of and no rule that derives it …
;;;
;;; `ein-cli/tests/import_left_behind.rs` holds the line.

(import upper_without_entry :symbols (upper))

(wanted a)

(solve :hypothesis-relations () :expect (region (model)))
(query :goal (got ?x ?y)
       :goal-text "{?x}'s double is {?y}"
       :expect (model))
